# Jobbliggaren edge — Caddy v2. ADR 0050 Option B: every request goes to Next.
# There is no /api matcher here on purpose; the ASP.NET API is not edge-exposed.
#
# BEFORE PUTTING THE API BEHIND THIS EDGE, read RecruiterErasureMatchQuery's
# CommandTimeoutSeconds. The Art. 17 erasure dry run runs under a reviewed ceiling that is
# larger than the `write` timeout below, and it reaches the API over the internal network
# today precisely because there is no /api matcher here. Route it through this edge and the
# request is capped at `write` instead, on the only human gate before an irreversible erase.
# That constant owns the rule and the measurement; this is a pointer, not a second copy.
#
# Values arrive as container environment (deploy/docker-compose.yml, from the box's
# deploy/.env). No credential is committed: this repo is public, and the basic-auth
# gate is the whole of admission control (gate M-5b point 5).
{
	email {$ACME_EMAIL}
	# Let's Encrypt production by default; point ACME_CA at the staging directory to
	# prove issuance without spending production rate limit. Both HTTP-01 and
	# TLS-ALPN-01 stay enabled — Caddy picks between them and falls back silently, so
	# disabling one is how you PROVE the other works, never how you run.
	acme_ca {$ACME_CA}

	servers {
		# Slowloris and slow-body defence. With no CDN in front, these timeouts and the
		# body cap below are the only availability controls the stack has (M-5b point 7).
		timeouts {
			read_body 10s
			read_header 5s
			write 30s
			idle 2m
		}
		# HTTP/3 is not advertised: the edge firewall passes TCP only, so an Alt-Svc
		# header would point clients at a UDP port that silently never answers.
		protocols h1 h2
	}
}

{$SITE_HOST} {
	# CHALLENGE-TYPE SEAM (#196). Default `both` resolves to an EMPTY file, so the running
	# configuration is byte-for-byte what it was before this line existed — Caddy's default
	# automation policy, both challenges enabled, adaptive fallback.
	#
	# The other two values exist because verification-log rows 5 and 6 cannot be taken without
	# them. Caddy picks between HTTP-01 and TLS-ALPN-01 and falls back SILENTLY, so one
	# certificate proves nothing about which path carried it — and a renewal path that was
	# never proven dies quietly about 60 days after cutover. Disabling one is how you PROVE
	# the other, never how you run.
	#
	# The key is PERMANENT (CTO 2026-08-05). The certificate renews forever and the renewal
	# path can be broken later by a firewall change, a Caddy upgrade or a DNS move; this is a
	# diagnostic instrument, not cutover scaffolding. Removing it would mean re-deriving and
	# re-deploying the mechanism on a box that is, at that moment, down.
	#
	# SINGLE COLON, not `:-`. Caddy splits env substitution on the FIRST colon, so `:-both`
	# makes the default the literal string `-both` and the import fails on `-both.caddy`.
	# Compose uses `:-` for the same job one layer out, and that IS correct there — the two
	# syntaxes look alike and are not. Measured: with the variable unset, `:-` gives
	# "File to import not found: /etc/caddy/challenge/-both.caddy" and exit 1, which behind
	# `restart: unless-stopped` is a crash-loop on the only service that publishes 80/443.
	import /etc/caddy/challenge/{$ACME_CHALLENGE_MODE:both}.caddy

	encode zstd gzip

	# Gate M-5a. This is the load-bearing half: the 401 below is answered here and
	# never reaches Next, so a header set only by buildSecurityHeaders would be absent
	# from the first response a browser sees. The value is byte-identical to the
	# STRICT_TRANSPORT_SECURITY constant in the web app.
	header Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"

	request_body {
		max_size 20MB
	}

	# THE EDGE OWNS THE ACME PREFIX; it is not merely exempted from the gate. Caddy
	# answers a live HTTP-01 challenge internally, ahead of site routing, so this block
	# never sees one. What it does see is every OTHER path under the prefix — and an
	# exemption alone would send those through to Next unauthenticated.
	#
	# Measured on the built image with a real upstream behind it: with `basic_auth`
	# merely scoped by `not path`, `GET /.well-known/acme-challenge/probe.txt` returned
	# application content with no credentials, carrying the upstream's own `Server`
	# header — proof the request was proxied rather than answered at the edge. Under
	# Option B that is Next running middleware, routing and i18n on unauthenticated
	# input, and K2 is the whole of admission control (gate M-5b point 5).
	#
	# The earlier form reasoned correctly about being wrong in one direction (a renewal
	# that dies silently about 60 days after cutover) and never considered the other,
	# which is the one that happened.
	handle /.well-known/acme-challenge/* {
		respond 404
	}

	handle {
		# THIS DIRECTIVE NOW CARRIES A GDPR CONCLUSION, NOT ONLY ACCESS CONTROL (#734).
		# release-checklist.md re-graded the dead Art. 12 rights channel on 2026-08-16
		# against a measured operating state, and one of the measurements is that this site
		# answers 401 on every path. Remove or bypass this block — even briefly, even for a
		# demo — and the blackholed contact address becomes blocking in that same moment,
		# with nothing to warn: the finding is filed against a state that stopped being true.
		# The published channel gets a reader before this comes off, not after.
		#
		# A SECOND GDPR CONCLUSION LIVED HERE FOR ONE DAY AND IS NOW SPENT (#183 E5 →
		# FU-2b, both 2026-08-16). It read: the email arm went live before the site did, so
		# content-legal.json rows 47, 75 and 76 assert in the present tense that Jobbliggaren
		# sends no email and discloses nothing to any email provider, those statements are
		# measured false, and removing this block would PUBLISH three false transparency
		# statements about a live processing (ADR 0090 D3).
		#
		# Struck rather than deleted, because a reader must be able to see it existed: for one
		# day this directive carried a transparency risk on top of access control. It is gone
		# because the rows were FIXED, not because the risk was re-judged — Klas chose väg A
		# and the copy was flipped to be true. Removing this block no longer publishes
		# anything false.
		#
		# What survives is the row that was always true and must stay marked: the consent-gated
		# watch notifications, which are still dark. If it ever goes false while this block is
		# still here, the second conclusion comes back and BOTH surfaces get it
		# again — this one and release-checklist.md §2.5 point 1 precondition 5, which owns the
		# grading. A fix on one of the two is no fix, in either direction.
		#
		# NAMED BY ITS TEXT AND NOT BY A LINE NUMBER, AND THAT IS THE REPAIR (#183, 2026-08-28).
		# This comment said "row 64" from 2026-08-16 until then. The row moved to 82 on
		# 2026-08-19, and the sweep that re-measured its twelve other homes did not reach this
		# file — so a thirteenth home pointed nine days at a paragraph about something else.
		# release-checklist.md section 2.6 point 1 already owns the rule that was broken: sweep
		# on the property, never on the file you happen to have open. Regenerate with one
		# command PER LOCALE, because the two strings differ and a single Swedish command
		# returns zero on the English file:
		#   grep -n "Notiserna planeras att skickas med e-post" web/jobbliggaren-web/messages/sv/content-legal.json
		#   grep -n "The notifications are planned to be sent by email" web/jobbliggaren-web/messages/en/content-legal.json
		basic_auth {
			{$BASIC_AUTH_USER} {$BASIC_AUTH_HASH}
		}

		# lb_try_duration IS THE ONE THAT MATTERS AT DEPLOY TIME, and it was missing.
		# Measured: caddy has no depends_on (deliberately — see docker-compose.yml), so on
		# `up -d` it can win the race against web. Its first active health check then gets
		# connection refused, the upstream is marked down, and with a 30 s interval the site
		# returns 503 for up to half a minute after everything is already healthy. That is a
		# visible outage on every reconcile.
		#
		# lb_try_duration makes a request WAIT and retry instead of failing instantly, and
		# the shorter interval shrinks the window behind it. Proven: upstream started after
		# caddy, request served 200 rather than 503.
		#
		# THE THREE NUMBERS ARE BOUND BY ONE INEQUALITY: health_interval + health_timeout
		# must stay under lb_try_duration, because that sum is the worst-case time to
		# notice the upstream came back. 5 + 3 < 10 leaves two seconds. Raise either of the
		# first two without the third and the boot-race 503 returns intermittently.
		#
		# AND THE TRADE IS REAL IN THE OTHER DIRECTION: during a genuine web outage every
		# request now blocks for up to 10 s instead of failing fast, so caddy holds roughly
		# rate x 10s concurrent connections where it held none. Bounded here because
		# basic_auth runs BEFORE the proxy in this same handle block, so an unauthenticated
		# flood gets 401 and never reaches the retry loop.
		#
		# Retrying is safe for non-idempotent methods by Caddy's default and not by luck: a
		# retry after the connection itself failed sends nothing upstream, and the case
		# where a round-trip failed after connecting is GET-only unless lb_retry_match says
		# otherwise. Do not add lb_retry_match without revisiting that.
		reverse_proxy web:3000 {
			# THE ONLY WRITER OF THIS HEADER IN THE STACK, and pinning that is the point.
			# Since 2.5 Caddy already ignores an inbound X-Forwarded-For from an untrusted
			# peer and sets it to the TCP client address, and no `trusted_proxies` is
			# configured here — so this line is behaviour-neutral today. It is written down
			# because the docs reserve the right to stop setting these headers by default,
			# and a silent default change would re-sever the chain #1202 exists to close,
			# with no signal. `{client_ip}` is trusted_proxies-aware, so inserting a CDN
			# later keeps the single-value contract without touching the ForwardLimit — but only via the
			# GLOBAL `servers > trusted_proxies`, which is what enables client-IP parsing. The
			# reverse_proxy subdirective governs only this handler's own X-Forwarded-* augmentation and
			# would leave {client_ip} resolving to the CDN.
			header_up X-Forwarded-For {client_ip}
			# X-Forwarded-Proto for the same reason and by the same argument. Doubly inert today —
			# Caddy overwrites it for an untrusted peer, and nothing in the API reads Request.Scheme
			# (UseHsts and UseHttpsRedirection are both off under Option B) — but pinning one header
			# of a pair and leaving the other on a default is an asymmetry with no reason behind it.
			header_up X-Forwarded-Proto {scheme}
			health_uri /api/health
			health_interval 5s
			health_timeout 3s
			lb_try_duration 10s
			lb_try_interval 500ms
		}
	}
}
